1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,793
The night of July 15th, 1953. North of

2
00:00:03,813 --> 00:00:06,797
the Imjin River, in Yeoncheon, Korea.

3
00:00:06,856 --> 00:00:08,749
Just twelve days remained before

4
00:00:08,769 --> 00:00:10,520
the guns would fall silent.

5
00:00:10,947 --> 00:00:14,439
On a small hill across the river, Outpost Betty,

6
00:00:14,459 --> 00:00:17,660
a single South Korean platoon held on.

7
00:00:17,782 --> 00:00:19,949
And in the darkness, Chinese troops

8
00:00:19,969 --> 00:00:21,794
were crawling up its slopes.

9
00:00:22,233 --> 00:00:24,216
Thirty-four South Korean soldiers,

10
00:00:24,236 --> 00:00:27,322
against a Chinese force of battalion strength.

11
00:00:27,403 --> 00:00:29,580
Their platoon leader, Kim Man-sul,

12
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:32,666
decided he would not abandon the position.

13
00:00:33,251 --> 00:00:34,910
A night-long grenade battle,

14
00:00:34,930 --> 00:00:37,983
and shells called down on their own heads.

15
00:00:38,069 --> 00:00:40,781
When dawn came, South Korean soldiers

16
00:00:40,801 --> 00:00:42,545
were still standing on the hill.

17
00:00:42,970 --> 00:00:45,829
One last stand for a sliver of ground,

18
00:00:45,849 --> 00:00:48,094
on the eve of the armistice.

19
00:00:48,210 --> 00:00:49,248
This is the story of the

20
00:00:49,268 --> 00:00:50,890
Battle of Outpost Betty.

21
00:00:51,331 --> 00:00:54,220
By the summer of 1953, two years of

22
00:00:54,240 --> 00:00:56,792
armistice talks were nearing their end.

23
00:00:56,902 --> 00:00:58,941
Both sides launched final attacks

24
00:00:58,961 --> 00:01:00,479
across the front, fighting

25
00:01:00,499 --> 00:01:02,428
for every last foot of ground.

26
00:01:02,852 --> 00:01:04,627
For nearly two years, the front

27
00:01:04,647 --> 00:01:06,926
line had barely moved at all.

28
00:01:07,045 --> 00:01:08,220
All along the line,

29
00:01:08,240 --> 00:01:11,146
hills changed hands again and again.

30
00:01:11,579 --> 00:01:14,211
On July 13th, the Chinese launched

31
00:01:14,231 --> 00:01:15,782
a massive offensive around

32
00:01:15,802 --> 00:01:18,012
Kumsong on the central front.

33
00:01:18,150 --> 00:01:20,770
At the same time, attacks hit outposts

34
00:01:20,790 --> 00:01:22,852
across the western front as well.

35
00:01:23,283 --> 00:01:26,604
In Yeoncheon, held by the ROK 1st Division,

36
00:01:26,624 --> 00:01:29,130
the Imjin River makes a great bend.

37
00:01:29,268 --> 00:01:31,274
Across the river from the main line,

38
00:01:31,294 --> 00:01:34,625
a lone outpost hill jutted out.

39
00:01:35,040 --> 00:01:37,344
Betty stood about one kilometer forward of

40
00:01:37,364 --> 00:01:40,405
the main line, an eye watching the enemy.

41
00:01:40,530 --> 00:01:42,263
Lose it, and the positions south

42
00:01:42,283 --> 00:01:44,300
of the river would lie exposed.

43
00:01:44,732 --> 00:01:46,287
It was barely over a hundred meters

44
00:01:46,307 --> 00:01:48,700
high, but steep on every side.

45
00:01:48,827 --> 00:01:52,540
Chinese positions in front, the Imjin behind.

46
00:01:52,560 --> 00:01:54,473
There was nowhere to fall back.

47
00:01:54,922 --> 00:01:57,959
It was held by 2nd Platoon, 6th Company,

48
00:01:57,979 --> 00:02:02,443
2nd Battalion, 11th Regiment, ROK 1st Division.

49
00:02:02,590 --> 00:02:05,416
Its leader, Kim Man-sul, had enlisted in

50
00:02:05,436 --> 00:02:09,765
1947 and fought from Tabu-dong to Pyongyang.

51
00:02:10,205 --> 00:02:11,983
Just a day before the battle, he passed

52
00:02:12,003 --> 00:02:14,111
his battlefield commissioning exam.

53
00:02:14,234 --> 00:02:15,751
So Korean records list him as

54
00:02:15,771 --> 00:02:17,538
a second lieutenant, and U.S.

55
00:02:17,558 --> 00:02:19,518
records as a master sergeant.

56
00:02:19,665 --> 00:02:21,758
Whatever his rank, that night he was

57
00:02:21,778 --> 00:02:23,806
the leader of this small platoon.

58
00:02:24,234 --> 00:02:26,646
On the evening of July 15th, he led his

59
00:02:26,666 --> 00:02:29,343
men up Betty and took over the position.

60
00:02:29,462 --> 00:02:33,210
A small platoon, recorded as 34, 35,

61
00:02:33,230 --> 00:02:36,198
or 36 men depending on the source.

62
00:02:36,643 --> 00:02:39,065
That night, the shelling began.

63
00:02:39,171 --> 00:02:41,189
Chinese artillery and mortar rounds

64
00:02:41,209 --> 00:02:43,838
rained down on the tiny hilltop.

65
00:02:44,273 --> 00:02:45,850
Trenches caved in, and the

66
00:02:45,870 --> 00:02:48,092
phone line to the rear was cut.

67
00:02:48,167 --> 00:02:51,267
In an instant, the little hill became an island,

68
00:02:51,287 --> 00:02:53,570
cut off on the far side of the river.

69
00:02:54,018 --> 00:02:56,097
Then Chinese infantry began climbing

70
00:02:56,117 --> 00:02:58,111
the slopes from several directions.

71
00:02:58,166 --> 00:03:00,001
U.S. records say a battalion;

72
00:03:00,021 --> 00:03:02,713
Korean records say two battalions.

73
00:03:03,162 --> 00:03:04,614
Blowing bugles and whistles,

74
00:03:04,634 --> 00:03:07,401
the Chinese surged forward in the dark.

75
00:03:07,480 --> 00:03:08,916
Every time a flare burst,

76
00:03:08,936 --> 00:03:11,060
the slopes were full of shadows.

77
00:03:11,497 --> 00:03:12,963
As the enemy reached the edge of the

78
00:03:12,983 --> 00:03:15,649
trenches, a grenade battle broke out.

79
00:03:15,771 --> 00:03:17,459
Grenades flew back and forth at a

80
00:03:17,479 --> 00:03:19,723
distance where faces could be seen.

81
00:03:20,171 --> 00:03:22,227
Kim ran along the trench line,

82
00:03:22,247 --> 00:03:24,104
throwing grenades himself.

83
00:03:24,185 --> 00:03:26,829
His U.S. award citation records that

84
00:03:26,849 --> 00:03:29,453
his grenades killed six of the enemy.

85
00:03:29,890 --> 00:03:31,910
It states that he fought the enemy hand

86
00:03:31,930 --> 00:03:33,959
to hand through the night and stopped

87
00:03:33,979 --> 00:03:36,196
the position from being overrun.

88
00:03:36,631 --> 00:03:39,475
The enemy pulled back, then came again.

89
00:03:39,536 --> 00:03:42,339
The attacks went on all night, five to

90
00:03:42,359 --> 00:03:45,486
nineteen times, depending on the record.

91
00:03:45,906 --> 00:03:47,558
To restore communications,

92
00:03:47,578 --> 00:03:50,597
Staff Sergeant Lee Kang-ro reportedly ran

93
00:03:50,617 --> 00:03:53,032
through the shellfire to headquarters.

94
00:03:53,200 --> 00:03:54,808
In trenches out of ammunition,

95
00:03:54,828 --> 00:03:57,735
men fought with bayonets and rifle butts.

96
00:03:58,184 --> 00:04:00,717
One by one, the platoon's men fell.

97
00:04:00,841 --> 00:04:02,813
The survivors gathered the ammunition of

98
00:04:02,833 --> 00:04:05,213
the fallen and took their places again.

99
00:04:05,382 --> 00:04:08,334
Still, Kim would not give up the hill.

100
00:04:08,766 --> 00:04:10,580
As dawn approached, the enemy

101
00:04:10,600 --> 00:04:12,742
finally broke into the trenches.

102
00:04:12,818 --> 00:04:14,926
Kim seized the radio and made

103
00:04:14,946 --> 00:04:17,180
an almost unthinkable request.

104
00:04:17,596 --> 00:04:19,378
He asked his own artillery to drop

105
00:04:19,398 --> 00:04:21,585
shells right on top of his position.

106
00:04:21,724 --> 00:04:24,072
Fire on a hill where friend and foe were

107
00:04:24,092 --> 00:04:27,363
tangled together: fire on his own lines.

108
00:04:27,812 --> 00:04:29,431
Shells bursting overhead

109
00:04:29,451 --> 00:04:31,525
could kill his own men too.

110
00:04:31,640 --> 00:04:33,145
Even so, he chose that

111
00:04:33,165 --> 00:04:35,100
risk over losing the hill.

112
00:04:35,545 --> 00:04:37,683
The soldiers pressed themselves flat

113
00:04:37,703 --> 00:04:39,572
at the bottom of their trenches.

114
00:04:39,711 --> 00:04:42,354
Above them, friendly shells swept the

115
00:04:42,374 --> 00:04:45,114
Chinese off the slopes and the hilltop.

116
00:04:45,526 --> 00:04:47,950
When the barrage lifted, the survivors

117
00:04:47,970 --> 00:04:49,975
charged out of their trenches.

118
00:04:50,065 --> 00:04:51,240
They drove the remaining

119
00:04:51,260 --> 00:04:53,413
enemy back down the slopes.

120
00:04:53,540 --> 00:04:55,933
As dawn broke, Outpost Betty was

121
00:04:55,953 --> 00:04:59,049
once again in South Korean hands.

122
00:04:59,476 --> 00:05:01,698
Morning, July 16th.

123
00:05:01,758 --> 00:05:03,698
The night-long battle was over.

124
00:05:04,128 --> 00:05:06,671
A few exhausted soldiers stood on the hill.

125
00:05:06,691 --> 00:05:08,025
Their uniforms were caked

126
00:05:08,045 --> 00:05:09,550
with blood and dirt.

127
00:05:09,638 --> 00:05:12,669
Of the 34, only about twelve, including

128
00:05:12,689 --> 00:05:15,357
their leader, are said to have survived.

129
00:05:15,780 --> 00:05:18,599
Korean records count 314 Chinese

130
00:05:18,619 --> 00:05:22,423
killed and an estimated 450 wounded.

131
00:05:22,533 --> 00:05:23,662
Numbers hard to believe

132
00:05:23,682 --> 00:05:25,294
for a single platoon.

133
00:05:25,734 --> 00:05:28,483
Eleven days later, on July 27th,

134
00:05:28,503 --> 00:05:30,472
the armistice was signed.

135
00:05:30,609 --> 00:05:33,026
Betty ended up inside the Demilitarized

136
00:05:33,046 --> 00:05:35,540
Zone, between North and South.

137
00:05:35,580 --> 00:05:37,233
The ground they held to the last

138
00:05:37,253 --> 00:05:39,610
moment remains there to this day.

139
00:05:40,050 --> 00:05:41,697
How could one platoon hold off a

140
00:05:41,717 --> 00:05:44,154
battalion-size force all night?

141
00:05:44,260 --> 00:05:46,429
Let's break it down into four factors.

142
00:05:46,870 --> 00:05:48,629
First, steep ground.

143
00:05:49,065 --> 00:05:50,414
The enemy had to climb steep

144
00:05:50,434 --> 00:05:52,610
slopes, on a narrow front.

145
00:05:52,665 --> 00:05:54,569
The terrain kept large numbers

146
00:05:54,589 --> 00:05:56,161
from attacking at once.

147
00:05:56,216 --> 00:05:58,223
And with the Imjin at their backs,

148
00:05:58,243 --> 00:06:00,456
there was no road of retreat.

149
00:06:00,900 --> 00:06:02,632
Second, the grenade fight.

150
00:06:03,070 --> 00:06:04,956
In darkness and on steep slopes,

151
00:06:04,976 --> 00:06:07,117
the grenade was the deadliest weapon.

152
00:06:07,172 --> 00:06:08,484
The side throwing downhill

153
00:06:08,504 --> 00:06:10,042
had the clear advantage.

154
00:06:10,464 --> 00:06:13,110
Third, fire on his own position.

155
00:06:13,548 --> 00:06:15,004
The men flat in their trenches

156
00:06:15,024 --> 00:06:16,757
survived, while the enemy in

157
00:06:16,777 --> 00:06:18,919
the open took the full blast.

158
00:06:19,048 --> 00:06:20,724
Making himself the target was the

159
00:06:20,744 --> 00:06:23,175
decision that won the hill back.

160
00:06:23,606 --> 00:06:26,042
Fourth, a leader who stayed.

161
00:06:26,455 --> 00:06:28,203
Kim led his platoon from the most

162
00:06:28,223 --> 00:06:31,275
dangerous spot, throwing grenades himself.

163
00:06:31,364 --> 00:06:33,040
He was first to grab a grenade,

164
00:06:33,060 --> 00:06:35,077
and last to leave the trench.

165
00:06:35,173 --> 00:06:36,370
That is what kept even the

166
00:06:36,390 --> 00:06:38,030
last few men fighting.

167
00:06:38,473 --> 00:06:40,375
For this battle, Kim received Korea's

168
00:06:40,395 --> 00:06:42,799
Taeguk Order of Military Merit and

169
00:06:42,819 --> 00:06:45,314
the U.S. Distinguished Service Cross.

170
00:06:45,388 --> 00:06:48,225
The DSC is the U.S. Army's highest award

171
00:06:48,245 --> 00:06:50,520
for valor after the Medal of Honor.

172
00:06:50,961 --> 00:06:53,966
In 1956, a film about his story,

173
00:06:53,986 --> 00:06:56,298
'Repulse,' was even made.

174
00:06:56,387 --> 00:06:57,938
A bust of him still stands

175
00:06:57,958 --> 00:06:59,988
in Paju's Unification Park.

176
00:07:00,419 --> 00:07:02,277
U.S. Army General Orders No.

177
00:07:02,297 --> 00:07:06,067
35, dated May 13th, 1954.

178
00:07:06,150 --> 00:07:07,940
On the citation, his name is

179
00:07:07,960 --> 00:07:10,566
written as 'Man Soul Kim.'

180
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:14,363
After the war, a training accident in 1958

181
00:07:14,383 --> 00:07:16,844
left him paralyzed from the waist down.

182
00:07:17,040 --> 00:07:19,183
Retiring as a captain, he founded a

183
00:07:19,203 --> 00:07:21,844
school and cared for disabled veterans.

184
00:07:22,286 --> 00:07:25,915
In 1991, his wartime wounds flared

185
00:07:25,935 --> 00:07:28,157
up again, and he passed away.

186
00:07:28,243 --> 00:07:31,334
Today he rests in Seoul National Cemetery.

187
00:07:31,770 --> 00:07:33,780
Today, from the Taepung Observatory

188
00:07:33,800 --> 00:07:36,239
in Yeoncheon, you can see Outpost Betty

189
00:07:36,259 --> 00:07:39,619
across the Imjin, inside the DMZ.

190
00:07:39,754 --> 00:07:41,624
So close you could almost touch it,

191
00:07:41,644 --> 00:07:43,998
yet still a place no one can enter.

192
00:07:44,591 --> 00:07:45,914
More than seventy years have

193
00:07:45,934 --> 00:07:47,791
passed since that night.

194
00:07:47,880 --> 00:07:49,875
Few people today still remember the names

195
00:07:49,895 --> 00:07:52,488
of the thirty-four men who held that hill.

196
00:07:53,075 --> 00:07:57,140
Thirty-four men, and one long night.

197
00:07:57,160 --> 00:07:59,384
On that night, less than two weeks

198
00:07:59,404 --> 00:08:01,171
before the armistice, what they

199
00:08:01,191 --> 00:08:03,644
held was a sliver of their country.

200
00:08:04,048 --> 00:08:05,625
If this story meant something to you,

201
00:08:05,645 --> 00:08:07,786
please subscribe and like the video.

202
00:08:07,844 --> 00:08:08,928
We'll continue to bring the

203
00:08:08,948 --> 00:08:10,934
forgotten records of war to light.
